        <h2 id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">Who Should Skip This Treadmill</h2>
        <p>
          We like the F63 for exactly what it's built to do, but it isn't
          the right machine for every shopper who lands on this page. If
          you're training for a race, running most days of the week, or
          consistently pushing past a moderate jogging pace, the smaller
          motor and narrower deck will start to feel limiting — the
          <a href="/sole-f65-treadmill">Sole F65</a> is a better long-term
          fit and will save you from needing to upgrade again within a year
          or two. If interactive touchscreen coaching content matters to
          you, this LCD-console machine will disappoint regardless of price
          — look at Sole's own F80 or a NordicTrack model instead. And if
          your household includes anyone over the 325-lb weight capacity,
          you'll need to look at the F65 or another model with higher rated
          capacity for safety and long-term durability reasons.
        </p>

        <p>
          It's also worth a word for anyone shopping specifically for a
          second or backup treadmill for a garage gym, guest room, or
          vacation property — the F63's combination of real Sole build
          quality and a genuinely low price makes it a sensible pick for
          secondary use cases where the F65's extra motor headroom simply
          isn't necessary. We've heard from several readers who bought a
          higher-tier machine for their primary training and picked up an
          F63 for a second location specifically because it doesn't
          compromise on frame quality the way a true rock-bottom treadmill
          would.
        </p>

        <h2 id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">Final Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          The Sole F63 succeeds because it doesn't pretend to be something
          it isn't. It's an honest, well-built entry point into a respected
          treadmill brand, delivering genuine frame quality, real cushioning
          technology, and a lifetime warranty most budget competitors can't
          match — all without a subscription requirement. Where it falls
          short is exactly where you'd expect from an entry-level model: the
          motor and deck are sized for walking and light jogging, not
          serious daily running, and stretching it past that use case is
          where owners run into trouble.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you're shopping with a clear-eyed sense of how you'll actually
          use a treadmill — mostly walking, occasional light jogging, a
          budget that tops out under $1,200 — the F63 is one of the more
          honest values in this price bracket. If you already know you'll
          be running regularly, save yourself a future upgrade and start
          with the F65 instead.
